|~ `::tagged`, the step that puts a value under a tag, the refusals
   of a tag without a constructor, and the verbs of tagged values. Each resides on `::qlang/tagged`,
   the kind of every value under a tag, which the walk of a tagged value
   reaches after its own tag. ~|

::tagged
  |~~ The step of a constructor invocation, `::T<payload>`: it puts a
      payload under the tag `::T` through the tag's constructor, which
      checks or builds the value. As a record, `:tag` is the tag and
      `:payload` the step of its payload. ::link~(::tag | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :pipeline)

      ~(~(::Box[1 2]) | first | [/tag /payload] | eq [::Box [1 2]])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/tagged}

::TaggedPayloadNotSchemaError
  |~~ A ::tagged payload is a Map of `:tag` a tag and `:payload` a
      step.
      ::link~(::tagged | doc)

      ~(
        ::tagged{:tag :Box :payload [1]}
        !| [type /field]
        |  eq [::TaggedPayloadNotSchemaError :tag]
       )
  ~~|

::TaggedReadBackDiffersError
  |~~ The ::tagged prints as text that reads back as another step: a
      payload that opens with a name, a digit or a slash is written
      as a group.
      ::link~(::tagged | doc)

      ~(
        ::tagged{:tag ::Box :payload 42}
        !| [type /printed]
        |  eq [::TaggedReadBackDiffersError "::Box42"]
       )
  ~~|

::TagBindingHasNoConstructorError
  |~~ Tag-binding has no registered constructor — `:impl`
      carries an unexpected value (neither a `:qlang/prim/*`
      keyword nor a Quote-impl body). Descriptor fields:
      `:payloadValue` / `:payloadType`, `:actualValue` /
      `:actualType`, `:expectedType [:keyword :quote]`.
      ::link~(::tagged | doc)
  ~~|

::TaggedLitNotTagBindingError
  |~~ `::tag` constructor invocation — the env binding under
      `::tag` is not a tag-binding descriptor Map.
      ::link~(::tagged | doc)

      ~(
        ::badType 42 | ::badType[]
        !| [type /tag /actualType]
        |  eq [::TaggedLitNotTagBindingError :badType ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:payload
  |~~ The value the tag holds, the reverse of `tag`: `value | tag ::Foo
      | payload | eq value` holds of every value. A set's payload is its
      vector.

      ~(::Box {} | ::Box[42] | payload | eq [42])
      ~(::Pair {} | ::Pair[1 2] | payload | eq [1 2])
      ~(::Note {} | ::Note"hello" | payload | eq "hello")
      ~(#[2 1] | payload | eq [1 2])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/payload})

::PayloadSubjectNotTaggedInstanceError
  |~~ `payload` requires a TaggedInstance subject (a value built
      through a `::Tag<payload>` constructor invocation).
      ~(
        42 | payload
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::PayloadSubjectNotTaggedInstanceError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:within
  |~~ An edit under one tag: `tagged | within ~(…)` runs the quote
      against the payload as `apply` runs it, a fork whose declarations
      stay inside, and the answer returns the subject's kind, minted
      back under its tag, whose constructor runs once, at the rewrap.
      The steps between may break the tag's invariant,
      since an invariant holds of the result: a set's constructor
      normalizes the vector the edit answers, where a record's refuses
      it. An error the edit answers passes as it is, and a deeper stack
      of tags is reached by nesting `within`.

      ~(#[3 1] | within ~([/0 /1 /0]) | eq #[1 3])
      ~(::Box {} | ::Box[1 2] | within ~(reverse) | eq ::Box[2 1])
      ~(::Box {} | ::Box#[3 1] | within ~(within ~([/1 /0 2])) | eq ::Box#[1 2 3])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:code ::quote | :returns / | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/within})

::WithinSubjectNotTaggedInstanceError
  |~~ `within` requires a TaggedInstance subject, a value that has a
      tag to edit under.
      ~(
        [1 2] | within ~(reverse)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::WithinSubjectNotTaggedInstanceError ::vec]
       )
  ~~|

::WithinCodeNotQuoteError
  |~~ `within` requires its captured arg to answer a Quote — the
      edit it runs against the payload.
      ~(
        #[3 1] | within 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::WithinCodeNotQuoteError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
